## On the timeouts

You were right, and it wasn't a one-off. I was wrapping `run.sh` chunks in 420–520 s timeouts when the work took 26–76 s, and passing 10-minute values to the Bash tool. `run.sh` *already* enforces a RULE 0 budget per test; my outer wrapper was padding on top of an assertion that was being made correctly. I wrote it into `CLAUDE.md` RULE 0 (which is read every session start) and into a `feedback` memory (which surfaces in every session's first `memory_list`), with the derivation: `sum(measured walls) + 12 s × n_tests + 15 s startup`. That 12 s/test harness-overhead term was measured both ways — omitting it made a 193 s chunk overrun a 254 s wrapper; including it predicted 88 s for a chunk that ran 61 s.

## Two critical defects closed, both FIXED AND VERIFIED

**The SCSI-PR fence had never issued PREEMPT AND ABORT.** Root cause is an upstream Linux bug: `dm_pr_preempt()` accepts `bool abort` and never assigns it into the `struct dm_pr` it passes down — so `sd_pr_preempt()` always issues `0x04`, on every dm device, at 6.19-rc0 and on the running 6.8. MXFS asked for the abort, dm dropped it, and MXFS then minted a *durable certificate* asserting exclusion was PROVED — the certificate that authorises replaying a dead node's journal. Measured on the shipped handler: under `0x04` a held victim write lands **+12.0 s and +12.5 s after** the fence completes; under `0x05` it lands **126 µs before**. MXFS now issues the raw PROUT itself and fails closed when it can't. Verified on the wire by SCST's own CDB parser: 0.14.11 → `Preempt:`, 0.14.12 → `Preempt and abort:`, reproduced on two victim/prover pairs.

**Nothing validated that fencing worked until a peer had already died.** 0.15.0 issues REPORT CAPABILITIES at mount and refuses admission if the device can't fence. A loop device that used to mount read-write silently is now refused by name, or admitted only under an explicit operator override that logs its own weaker semantics.

## Also found

- **The PR fence has zero board coverage.** A full board produces no fence markers on any of 32 nodes — `fence_during_write` is a *negative* test that asserts nothing is fenced, and `crash_consistency` says in its own header it can't kill a node. The ledger's plan to get fence evidence from those tests was based on a false premise; `tests/fence_stage3_real.sh` is now the only thing that exercises the path.
- Filed `D-CREATE-METADATA-READ-EIO-NODE-SHUTDOWN-378` (a node died on a metadata read error and cascaded a green board red), and recorded a 302 s single-node unmount with an orphaned `ACQUIRING` state against the mass-unmount entry.

Full 32/caw board on 0.15.0: 27 pass, 0 fail, 1 policy cell.

